FerrionOS change to the Redox kernel (MIT license, kept), made against upstream commit e7f1d4231d0f4b55fbef229d9cd9459302c101f4 with the patches before it in the recipe's list applied (security roadmap S18, IOMMU plan slice 2, the DMA handle):
- `memory` scheme path `dma/SSSS-BB-DD.F` (hexadecimal segment, bus, device and function) opens the DMA handle of one PCI requester ID; only the unrestricted root and uid 0 may (EACCES), a second open of the same ID fails with EBUSY while the first lives, and at most 64 handles exist (ENFILE);
- opening it moves the device out of the one-to-one domain into an empty domain of its own: its bus gets a private copy of the shared context table, and the entry is made not present, invalidated, rewritten and invalidated again (domain id 0 under caching mode);
- `mmap` on the handle allocates zeroed, physically contiguous memory as `zeroed?phys_contiguous` does, takes a second reference on every frame and maps the frames read-write at their own addresses in the device's domain; the offset picks the memory type (0 write-back, 0x1000 uncacheable, 0x2000 write-combining, 0x3000 device memory), at most 256 mappings of at most 4 MiB per handle, and `MAP_SHARED` is refused;
- a call with metadata 1 and a virtual address answers the device address of a page mapped through the handle (ENOENT for any other page); metadata 2 with a device address and a length removes exactly one mapping, invalidates and waits, then drops the extra references;
- closing the handle puts the device back into the one-to-one domain, invalidates, frees the domain's tables and only then drops the remaining references, so a frame is never reused while the device can reach it; frames whose invalidation failed stay pinned and are counted as `pinned-forever`;
- refused requests are attributed to the handle of their requester ID, and `/scheme/sys/iommu` lists each handle as `SSSS:BB:DD.F domain N pages P faults F` (`domain none` without a unit).

